```config
/***
  author: Me
  description: A few examples of ways to use NR Markdown notebooks
  var: main_app = "TaT (production)"
***/
```

```nrql
/***
  title: Busiest app, last week
  var: top_app
  chart: billboard
***/
FROM Transaction
SELECT count(*)
FACET appName
SINCE 1 week ago
LIMIT 4
```

```nrql
/***
  title: Metrics for the busiest app
  chart: line
  attributes: stacked smoothed
***/
FROM Metric SELECT count(*) WHERE appName = '{{ results.top_app.rows[1].facet }}' SINCE 1 week ago TIMESERIES 1 day
```

## Hidden results

Since we can bind resulsts for later use, it's possible that you may want to run a query to use later but not care about seeing that intermediate result.

In those caess, you can use `chart: none`. If you also collapse the query section, only the cell header will be shown in the document.

```nrql
/***
title: Transactions for the busiest app
var: transactions
chart: none
***/

FROM Transaction SELECT count(*) AS count WHERE appName = '{{ results.top_app.rows[1].facet }}' SINCE 1 week ago
```

## Seeing "raw" NRQL results

Alternatively, you may not need to see a chart visualized, but rather would like to see the data yourself in-line. For that you can use the `json` chart type.

```nrql
/***
title: Errors for the busiest app
var: errors
chart: json
***/

FROM TransactionError SELECT count(*) WHERE appName = '{{ results.top_app.rows[1].facet }}' SINCE 1 week ago
```

## Running lua scripts

In this exmaple, two previous results (which have hidden graphs, but still run and save results) are referenced to build a result from the independent data sets.

The `emit` function is used to push data into the chartting system.

```lua
--[[
title: Transactions vs. errors
chart: bar
]]

emit({
  rows = {
    { facet = "Transaction", count = results.transactions.rows[1].count },
    { facet = "TransactionError", count = results.errors.rows[1].count },
  },
})
```

## Running Nerdgraph queries

We can run arbitrary Nerdgraph queries with a light GraphQL editor. Binding of results can be done generically with a `var: var_name` keyword or can be done with a short lua `key = value` accessor. 

```graphql

# title: Entity Search
# var: entity_search
# var: entities = data.actor.entitySearch.results.entities
# chart: none
query FindEntity($account_id: Int!, $query: String) {
  actor {
    account(id: $account_id) { name }
    entitySearch(query: $query) {
      results { entities {
        guid 
        account { name id }
        name
        reporting
        tags { key values }
      }}
    }
  }
}
```
```variables
{
  "account_id": {{ config.accounts[1] }},
  "query": "name = '{{ results.top_app.rows[1].facet }}'"
}
```

```lua
--[[
title: Entity tag count
chart: billboard
]]

local entity = assert(results.entities[1], "No matching entity found")

emit({
  rows = {
    { ["Tag Count"] = #entity.tags }
  }
})
```

## Using config variables

This query example shows how to use config block and global variables. In the config block, variables can be added which can be referred to as `config.var_name`

There are two intrinsic config variables as well:
* `config.accounts` (array, remember lua in 1-indexed!)
* `config.api_key` (only the name, not the key itself)

```graphql

# title: Entity Search
# var: entity_search
query FindEntity($account_id: Int!, $query: String) {
  actor {
    account(id: $account_id) {
      name
    }    
    entitySearch(query: $query) {
      results { entities {
        guid
        name
      }}
    }
  }
}
```
```variables
{
  "account_id": {{config.accounts[1]}},
  "query": "name = '{{ config.main_app }}'"
}
```
